  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In May 2021, during the COVID-19 pandemic, the National Confederation for Eritrean Workers (NCEW) in cooperation with Eritrean Federation of Employers (EFE) launched a national awareness campaign on aspects of Safe Return to Work. 

Through an innovative use of media in reaching out to workers nationally, including the radio channel &#x201c;Radio Numa&#x201d;, national TV, Local TV and EriTV, NCEW and EFE were able to provide new services to workers during the pandemic. 

Valuable information on up-to-date guidelines on how to ensure safe return to work &#x2013; from a worker perspective and an employer perspective to all workers nationwide was provided. 

The material used and broadcasted included two videos and posters in three languages (Arabic, Tigrigna, Tigre). The campaign promoted mandatory COVID-19 Workplace Safety Rules for Health Care Safety settings and recommended pre-cautionary measures for all industries and economic sectors. Workers in the food and beverage sector in particularly were better protected against the spread of COVID-19 due to this campaign as the provision of washing basins at the entrances, sanitizers, limits on seating (three per table), and Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) protection were introduced. Similarly, companies such as Melotti Share company or ZaER.plc continue to provide the necessary sanitation tools and PPE protection in their factories. 

A position paper on Safe Return to Work was developed and submitted to the Government in September 2021.  

Following the campaign, NCEW has developed a baseline and strategy paper on how to attract workers from the informal economy to join NCEW. With the support of the RBSA project in Eritrea, the National Confederation for Eritrean Workers (NCEW) was able reach out and provide better services to their members during the COVID-19 Panademic. 

The campaign used National Media as a platform for the best outreach, including radio channel &#x201c;Radio Numa&#x2019; and national TV Local TV, EriTV,.  The material that has been aired includes 2 shored videos, as well as production of posters, in three languages.

The Safe Return to Work Campaign, was a result of ILO support to a series of awareness webinars on impact of Covid-19 and safe return to work, 3 two days webinars for a total of 115 workers including members of OSH committees from 45 companies, and 4 days within one week for more than 40 employers representatives took place (February-March 2021). Furthermore, NCEW utilized 2 of the ILO guides, &#x201c;The 10 actions points and Ensuring safe return to work a guide for employers and workers&#x201d;, and translated to local different languages for dissemination. A position paper has also been developed on the same topic to be submitted to the government in September 2021.</narrative>
   </description>
